{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_23","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/eval_new/seed2085404187/3/codegen_prompts/2.md\"","aggregated_output":"你要为一个新的 ARC-AGI 风格题目实现代码。代码文件必须包含 `main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray` 和 `generate_input() -> np.ndarray`。\n\n## 新题位置\n\n- 描述目录：`/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed2085404187/3`\n- 题号：`2`\n- 目标代码路径：`/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/eval_new/seed2085404187/3/2.py`\n\n## 新题描述\n\n<new_puzzle index=\"2\">\n- `description`：将散落的彩色碎片按形状互补原则拼合成完整矩形，再根据拼合后矩形的面积大小关系确定输出排列顺序。\n\n- `Input structure`：\n  - 背景为 black。\n  - 网格中散布着若干彩色连通块（4-连通），每个连通块有自己的颜色（如 red、blue、green、yellow、magenta 等），形状不规则。\n  - 这些连通块可以两两配对：同一对中的两个块颜色不同，但形状互补——将其中一个块平移（不旋转、不翻转）后与另一个块恰好拼合成一个无间隙的实心矩形。\n  - 每个连通块恰好属于一对，不存在落单的块。\n\n- `Rule`：\n  - 第一层推理（配对）：找出哪些块可以互补拼成矩形。方法是：对每个块计算其最小外接矩形（bounding box），两个块能配对当且仅当它们的 bounding box 尺寸相同，且一个块的像素恰好填充另一个块在同尺寸 bounding box 中留下的空位。\n  - 第二层推理（排序与输出）：将配对后的矩形按面积从小到大排列。输出网格中，每个拼合矩形从左到右依次排列（矩形之间间隔 1 列 black），矩形内部用两个原始块的颜色填充——保留各自原来的颜色，拼合在一起。\n\n- `Key insight`：需要先通过\"形状互补\"这一非显然的几何关系找到正确配对，再通过面积排序决定输出布局。配对过程不是靠颜色或距离，而是靠形状的负空间匹配。\n</new_puzzle>\n\n## 机制相似参考题\n\n下面参考题由本地相似度流程自动选择：先用 `annotations`/docstring/代码特征抽取机制标签，再按机制相似度、文本相似度和代码特征相似度重排，并用 MMR 做去重。优先学习这些题的对象识别方式、随机生成器约束、边界条件处理，不要照搬旧题规则。\n\n已选参考：eval/62593bfd, eval/58490d8a, train/3ad05f52, eval/291dc1e1, eval/9385bd28, train/2601afb7, train/83b6b474, eval/a25697e4\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"1\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"62593bfd\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3948\nmechanism=0.4760\nlexical=0.0126\ncode_feature=0.5714\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, code:counter, code:rotation, object:sprite, operation:count, operation:rotate, operation:shape_match, operation:sort_order, operation:translate, relation:direction, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/62593bfd.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/62593bfd.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nSort objects to top or bottom edge by whether their shape (up to rotation/flip) is shared.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Several multicolor connected components (8-connected) scattered across the grid.\n\nRule:\n- Compute each object's canonical shape (binary mask, invariant to rotation and flip).\n- Objects whose shape appears in two or more components → translate to TOP edge (row 0).\n- Objects whose shape is unique → translate to BOTTOM edge (row H−h).\n- Horizontal position (left column) is preserved; only vertical translation changes.\n\nKey insight: The sorting criterion is shape frequency (rotation-invariant), not area — duplicated shapes go up, unique shapes go down.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_in_order\",\n  \"src\": \"all connected components (objects) on the background\",\n  \"dst\": \"translate each object d vertically to an edge (x preserved)\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"group objects by their pixel-count (area). If an area value occurs >=2 times, those objects go to the TOP edge; if the area is unique, that object goes to the BOTTOM edge\",\n  \"transform\": \"duplicated-size -> top; singleton-size -> bottom\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"2\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"58490d8a\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3753\nmechanism=0.5025\nlexical=0.0182\ncode_feature=0.2500\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, object:rectangle, object:sprite, operation:count, operation:sort_order, operation:translate, relation:between, relation:direction, relation:inside, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/58490d8a.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/58490d8a.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nCount colored objects and record tallies inside a legend box.\n\nInput structure:\n- A black rectangular legend box with key-color pixels in its leftmost non-black column.\n- Outside the box, distinct objects of each key color are scattered across the grid.\n\nRule:\n- For each key color, count separate same-colored objects outside the legend box.\n- Output the legend box repainted: each key row gets one dot per object, spaced every other cell rightward from the key pixel.\n\nKey insight: The legend is both the color index and the output canvas — object counts become dot sequences on the matching row.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_to_match\",\n  \"src\": \"colored sprites s outside the black legend bbox\",\n  \"dst\": \"follow pixels d inside the black legend bbox (one per key color)\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"s.color == d.color\",\n  \"transform\": \"s reduces to 1 dot placed left-to-right starting at pos(d) with 1 bg pixel gap, replacing d\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"3ad05f52\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3442\nmechanism=0.4017\nlexical=0.0125\ncode_feature=0.5714\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:bfs, code:connected_components, code:counter, object:rectangle, object:sprite, operation:bfs_distance, operation:sort_order, operation:translate, relation:between, relation:inside\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/3ad05f52.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/3ad05f52.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nPaint the maze corridor and framed rooms lying on shortest paths between the two colored endpoint components.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has a dominant background, one wall color forming thin orthogonal frames, and one endpoint/fill color.\n- The endpoint/fill color forms exactly two 4-connected solid rectangular components; their larger side lengths define the framed room sizes.\n\nRule\n- Treat wall pixels as blocked and every other pixel as passable.\n- Find every passable pixel that lies on a shortest 4-connected path between the two endpoint components.\n- Also fill any framed square room of an endpoint-derived size that intersects that shortest-path mask.\n\nKey insight\n- Shortest paths identify the route through open connector corridors, while the wall-bordered square rooms need to be expanded from the route centerline to their full interior.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_along_path\",\n  \"src\": \"one of two same-color endpoint components\",\n  \"dst\": \"the other same-color endpoint component\",\n  \"path\": \"shortest 4-connected route through non-wall pixels\",\n  \"trajectory\": \"paint background pixels on the route with the endpoint color\",\n  \"transform\": \"fill any wall-framed square room of an endpoint-derived size that the route crosses\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"4\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"291dc1e1\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3298\nmechanism=0.4134\nlexical=0.0183\ncode_feature=0.3750\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, code:rotation, object:rectangle, operation:bfs_distance, operation:rotate, operation:sort_order, operation:translate, relation:inside, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/291dc1e1.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/291dc1e1.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nReorder and stack rectangles by proximity to two colored edge lines.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Two edge lines (h + v) meet at a corner marked by a unique color.\n- \"Blue\" line is color 1 if present, else the longer line; other is \"red.\"\n- Solid rectangles in the interior.\n\nRule:\n- Sort rectangles by (distance to blue edge, distance to red edge).\n- If the blue edge is vertical, rotate each rectangle 90° CCW.\n- Stack top-to-bottom, centered horizontally.\n\nKey insight: Edge-line colors encode a coordinate system; proximity to each axis determines stacking order and rotation.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_in_order\",\n  \"src\": \"rectangles\",\n  \"dst\": \"into slot center aligned\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"(distance(s, blue_edge), distance(s, red_edge)).order == d.pos.row.order\",\n  \"transform\": \"rot90 if blue edge is vertical\",\n  \"multi_step\": \"move rectangles in order, one below another\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"5\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"9385bd28\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3284\nmechanism=0.4340\nlexical=0.0162\ncode_feature=0.2500\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:counter, object:rectangle, object:sprite, operation:bfs_distance, operation:count, operation:flood_fill, operation:sort_order, operation:translate, relation:between, relation:direction, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/9385bd28.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/9385bd28.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nFill rectangles between diagonal marker pairs using a color legend.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg grid with a vertical legend at the bottom-left: each row is a (marker_color, fill_color) pair.\n- Scattered L-shaped corner markers of each marker color define opposite corners of axis-aligned rectangles.\n- Some markers are solid 2×2 blocks instead of corner pairs.\n\nRule:\n- Read legend bottom-to-top; for each entry find all pixels of that marker color.\n- Determine which two bbox corners carry L-shaped anchors — the *other* diagonal defines the fill region.\n- Fill the diamond (all shortest-path cells between those two corners) with the legend's fill color.\n- Solid 2×2 marker blocks are simply recolored to the fill color.\n- Later legend entries are protected from earlier fills.\n\nKey insight: The L-shaped anchors sit on the marker diagonal; the fill goes on the opposite diagonal of the bounding box.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"summary\": \"Use a color legend at bottom-left to find diagonal marker pairs of matching color, then fill the rectangle between them with the adjacent legend color.\",\n  \"type\": \"move_to_match\",\n  \"src\": \"diagonal pixel pair s with same color forming opposite corners of a rectangle (upper corner pixel)\",\n  \"dst\": \"diagonal pixel pair d with same color forming opposite corners of a rectangle (lower corner pixel)\",\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
